  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Sundaramahalingam vs The Food Inspector on 3 July, 2019 Equivalent citations: Aironline 2019 Mad 449 Author: G.R.Swaminathan Bench: G.R.Swaminathan 1 Before The Madurai Bench Of Madras High Court Dated: 03.07.2019 Coram: The Honourable Mr.Justice G.R.Swaminathan Crl.R.C(MD)No.902 of 2011 Sundaramahalingam ... Petitio Vs The Food Inspector, Corporation of Tirunelveli, Tirunnelveli. ... Respond Prayer: Petition filed under Section 397 r/w 401 of Criminal Procedure Code, to call for the re cords of the Additional Sessions Judge, Ftc-Ii, Tirunelveli in C.A.No.70 of 2011 by Judgment dated 04.08.2011, confirming the conviction and sentenc imprisonment imposed by the learned Judicial Magistrate No.IV, Tirunelveli in Stc No.423 of 2007, by the Judgment date 01.03.2011 and [LEAKAGE_MASK] the conviction sentence imposed by the Courts below and acquit the petitioner. For Petitioner : Mr.Karunanithi for Mr.M.Karthikeya Venkitacha Respondent : Mr.A.Robinson Government Advocate (Crl.s Order",
    "facts": "The Food Inspector Corporation of Tirunelveli filed private http://www.judis.nic.incomplaint against one Narayanan and the revision petitioner Sundaramahalingam, under Section 7(ii) and 16 (1)(a)(i) r/w 2(ix) (k) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 and Rule 32(e) and (f) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1955. 2.The case of the prosecution/complainant is that on 18.08.2006 at about 12.00 p.m., in East Car Street, Tirunelveli, he saw the first accused Narayanan selling food articles in his shop Rajagomathi Stores. The complainant introduced himself as Food Inspector and enquired about the Vaigai Dates sold by Narayanan. The said Narayanan informed the complainant that he was having with him about 20 packets of Vaigai Dates, each packet containing 500 grams. Narayanan was having license from the Tirunelveli Corporation to sell the same. When he was enquired who had supplied those dates, the said Narayanan informed that the dates packets were sold by Annalakshmi Stores. It is seen that the Annalakshmi Stores was being run by the revision petitioner herein. The Food Inspector, thereupon, seized the articles and sent them for analysis by the food analyst. The Analyst submitted a report. After complying with the formalities under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954, the complainant came to the conclusion that the sample was misbranded and not labelled in accordance with Rule 32(e) and (f) of the Prevention of Food http://www.judis.nic.inAdulteration Rules, 1955. Based on the same, the Food Inspector filed the impugned complaint in S.T.C.No.4743 of 2007 on the file of the Judicial Magistrate No.4, Tirunelveli. The accused denied the charges framed against them and claimed to be tried. The prosecution examined two witnesses and marked Ex.P1 to Ex.P22. On the side of the accused, no evidence was adduced. It is relevant to note here that in the said Judgment, it was mentioned that the food articles sold by the accused was a good quality article. Aggrieved by the Judgment passed by the trial Magistrate, the petitioner herein- Sundaramahalingam filed Criminal Appeal No.70 of 2011 before the Additional Sessions Court, Fast Track Court-II, Tirunelveli. Challenging the same, this revision case came to be filed. 3.When the matter was taken up for hearing, the learned http://www.judis.nic.incounsel appearing for the petitioner submitted the only offence said to have been committed by the petitioner was one of misbranding. If that be so, the Food Inspector, even at the time of seizure of the food articles ought to have issued a notice in Form IV.",
